<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Automation on Mornati Blog</title><link>https://blog.mornati.net/tags/automation/</link><description>Recent content in Automation on Mornati Blog</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en-US</language><copyright>© 2026 Marco Mornati</copyright><lastBuildDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://blog.mornati.net/tags/automation/rss.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Laya on an N100: Swapping Jev for a Local Model in Home Assistant, and Teaching It My House with Hindsight</title><link>https://blog.mornati.net/laya-on-an-n100-replacing-jev-in-home-assistant-and-fine-tuning-it/</link><pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.mornati.net/laya-on-an-n100-replacing-jev-in-home-assistant-and-fine-tuning-it/</guid><description>A Jev-compatible model running on a 4-core mini PC, with none of my sensor data leaving the house. Out of the box it said &amp;ldquo;yes&amp;rdquo; to almost everything. Trained on 2,019 real decisions judged with hindsight, it ties Jev on the ventilation and gets summer shutters right 90% of the time, a decision Jev was never asked about. Along the way I found that Jev isn&amp;rsquo;t the ground truth either.</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blog.mornati.net/laya-on-an-n100-replacing-jev-in-home-assistant-and-fine-tuning-it/cover.jpg"/></item><item><title>Use AI to Build the Tool, Not to Be the Tool</title><link>https://blog.mornati.net/use-ai-to-build-the-tool-not-to-be-the-tool/</link><pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 05:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.mornati.net/use-ai-to-build-the-tool-not-to-be-the-tool/</guid><description>Asking an agent to block focus time, summarise yesterday&amp;rsquo;s numbers or find a meeting slot works, but you pay for it every time, and the answer changes. I measured 14 Claude Code runs on three everyday jobs. Writing the script once costs about as much as asking once. With an interactive calculator, a checklist, and my own projects as examples.</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blog.mornati.net/use-ai-to-build-the-tool-not-to-be-the-tool/cover.jpg"/></item><item><title>Drowning in Email: ai-email-assistant, Rules First, Models Last</title><link>https://blog.mornati.net/drowning-in-email-ai-email-assistant-rules-first-models-last/</link><pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 04:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.mornati.net/drowning-in-email-ai-email-assistant-rules-first-models-last/</guid><description>A Gmail triage that costs cents. Deterministic rules handle the routine mail, Claude Haiku looks only at what&amp;rsquo;s left, Claude Sonnet only at what Haiku isn&amp;rsquo;t sure about, and code validates every answer. Requests become Google Tasks with a due date. With a real run on my inbox, a from-zero install with Homebrew, and a few thoughts on letting an AI answer your mail.</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blog.mornati.net/drowning-in-email-ai-email-assistant-rules-first-models-last/cover.jpg"/></item><item><title>Jev in Home Assistant, Take Two: History, Feedback, and Why I Didn't Go Local</title><link>https://blog.mornati.net/jev-home-assistant-history-feedback-and-the-local-model-question/</link><pubDate>Sat, 26 Sep 2026 15:50:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.mornati.net/jev-home-assistant-history-feedback-and-the-local-model-question/</guid><description>The fix for &amp;ldquo;Jev has no memory&amp;rdquo; was never a different model. It was giving Jev a memory. Here is the history script, the feedback loop, and what the live scoreboard already disagrees about.</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blog.mornati.net/jev-home-assistant-history-feedback-and-the-local-model-question/cover.jpg"/></item><item><title>Jev in Home Assistant: Letting a Decision Model Make the Judgement Calls</title><link>https://blog.mornati.net/jev-home-assistant-letting-a-decision-model-judge-my-automations/</link><pubDate>Fri, 25 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.mornati.net/jev-home-assistant-letting-a-decision-model-judge-my-automations/</guid><description>My automations are good at thresholds and bad at context. Jev answers typed questions with a calibrated probability, for about two thousandths of a cent each. Here is how I wired it into Home Assistant with the old rules kept as a safety net, and what it really costs.</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blog.mornati.net/jev-home-assistant-letting-a-decision-model-judge-my-automations/cover.jpg"/></item><item><title>The Sump Pump in the Spotlight: Monitoring Hidden Infrastructure with Home Assistant</title><link>https://blog.mornati.net/monitoring-the-sump-pump-with-home-assistant/</link><pubDate>Tue, 25 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.mornati.net/monitoring-the-sump-pump-with-home-assistant/</guid><description>A Zigbee smart plug, nine automations, and one invisible pump that saves the house from flooding - the &amp;ldquo;invisible device&amp;rdquo; pattern, monitored with Home Assistant.</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blog.mornati.net/monitoring-the-sump-pump-with-home-assistant/cover.jpg"/></item><item><title>The Smart Water Heater, 4 Years Later: Solar + Off-Peak Orchestration (v2)</title><link>https://blog.mornati.net/smart-water-heater-orchestration-solar-off-peak-v2/</link><pubDate>Mon, 24 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.mornati.net/smart-water-heater-orchestration-solar-off-peak-v2/</guid><description>Two water heaters on a Heures Creuses tariff, 12 panels and no battery - the v2 of my water heater orchestra